1 CONVERT
    The CONVERT command copies the records of up to 10 files, one after
    another, to one file, changing their organization and format to the
    output file's.  It makes the output file from an FDL file, or else
    with the first input file's attributes, unless told to use one that's
    there.  An indexed output is sorted by its primary key, and loaded fast
    by building its indexes directly.  A host file can be an input, each
    line a record, but the output is a volume file.

    Syntax:

        CONVERT [qualifiers] <input>[,<input>...] <output>

    Parameters:

        <input>         A file to read; no wildcards.

        <output>        The file to write; no wildcards.  Without a type,
                        it takes the first input's.

    Qualifiers:

        /FDL=<file>     Make the output from this FDL file (default type
                        .FDL).  Any warning from it stops the conversion.

        /NOCREATE       Use an existing output file.
        /APPEND         Add to the end of an existing sequential file.
        /MERGE          Insert into an existing indexed file by its keys.

        /EXCEPTIONS_FILE[=<file>]
                        Copy each record that can't go in to this file
                        (default type .EXC), or with no file show it.
        /EXIT           End at the first such record.

        /PAD[=c]        Fill short records of a fixed-length file with c, a
                        character or %Dn, %On, or %Xn; null by default.
        /TRUNCATE       Cut off records too long for the output.
        /FIXED_CONTROL  Between VFC and other records, move the fixed control
                        to or from the data, rather than dropping it.

        /KEY=n          Read an indexed input in the order of key n.
        /NOSORT         The input is in order already.
        /NOFAST_LOAD    Load an indexed output by RMS's $PUT.
        /FILL_BUCKETS   Fill its buckets, whatever its FDL's fills.
        /PROLOG=3       Only Prolog 3 is available: 1 or 2 is RMS$_PLV.
        /STATISTICS     Show the counts and the time taken.

        /READ_CHECK, /WRITE_CHECK, and /WORK_FILES=n are taken and do nothing
        (n past 10 is SORT's BAD_MERGE); /SHARE reads the input shared.

    A record that can't go in is reported and passed over, and CONVERT goes
    on: %CONVERT-I-DUP (a duplicate key), RSK (shorter than the primary
    key), RTL (too long), RTS (too short for fixed records), SEQ (out of
    order under /NOSORT), or VFC (too short to fill the fixed control).

    Example:

        $ CONVERT/FDL=PARTS/EXCEPTIONS_FILE=BAD PARTS.TXT PARTS.IDX

    See also the help for CREATE /FDL.
